        rules: {
          objective: 'The aim of the play is to take tricks containing valuable cards and to declare King-Ober marriages; the first team that reaches 101 or more points wins the round and must stop the play by calling Aus to claim the win.',
          gameplay: 'Gaigel proceeds through deal, legal play actions, and end-of-round resolution. Play proceeds through legal actions until round resolution.',
          keyRules: [
            'Out! (Schluss!): A player can stop the game midndhand if they believe their team has reached 101 points. If they are correct, they win. If they are wrong, the opponents win regardless of the final score.',
            'Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Points scored through tricks and marriage declarations.',
            'Winning condition: First team to reach 101 points wins the round.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "In Phase 1, use your 7s to throw away junk cards and win tricks with your Aces to draw fresh cards from the Talon. Don't waste high cards until the Talon is nearly empty.",
          intermediate: "Count the 10s. Since 10s are worth 10 points but rank below Aces, they are easy to lose if you lead them too early. Try to 'duck' tricks that contain 10s unless you are sure you can win.",
          advanced: "Phase 2 Preparation: Watch which cards your partner is 'throwing' during Phase 1. If they throw a 7 of Hearts, it's a signal to play Hearts in Phase 2 because they are likely void or holding trumps to protect that suit.",
          tips: [
            'If you have a Trump Marriage (K+O), declare it immediately on your lead. 40 points is nearly 40% of the way to victory.',
            "Know when to stop the game. If you've been tracking tricks and know you are at 101, call 'Out!' before your opponent can catch up.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
